WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.670
Do you ever look down at your favourite snack
and think; how did we get here?

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.459
In just a few thousand years we’ve gone
from hunting mammoth to arguing over pizza

00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:11.499
toppings.

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:15.360
Take something like a cheeseburger, that seems
like a pretty simple thing, right?

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.750
But think about everything that needed to
happen in order for you to destroy your waistline

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.880
with bovine deliciousness.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.029
We needed to make bread that rises, domesticate
cattle, extract sugar for the ketchup.

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:31.890
And this is before we even get onto cheese;
who worked out that squeezing a cow’s nipples

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.910
and letting it go mouldy would turn out to
be one of the greatest joys in the universe?&nbsp;

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.920
Let’s be honest; food…is weird.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:47.460
So let’s look at 6 things that completely
changed how we eat.&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.010
Obviously the first major step was when we
learnt how to control fire.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:57.140
Despite what raw food enthusiasts may tell
you, cooking is vital to our development as

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.230
a species.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.460
The chemical changes that it creates enable
us to absorb a far higher amount of useful

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.100
energy, which is how we were able to make
the evolutionary jump to having such gigantic

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.540
brains, which gobble energy even if you’re
just sitting there staring at the wall; you

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.870
know; like Friday nights.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:19.960
To give you some examples, the body absorbs
about 30% more energy from cooked oats and

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:26.970
wheat than when raw, and a whopping 78% more
from a cooked egg compared to raw eggs, so

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:31.830
maybe stop drinking mayonnaise and just have
a fry up instead, like a normal person.&nbsp;

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:36.200
The process of cooking, including things like
chopping and grinding as well as heating,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.130
means your digestive system does far less
work to extract energy since you’ve already

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.340
broken down the meat’s connective tissues
or softened the plant's cell walls.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.620
You’re basically outsourcing that work so
the little people inside you can take a bit

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.799
of a break and a finally have that meeting
about what that weird lump on you shoulder

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:54.799
is.&nbsp;

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:58.210
After meat, one of the oldest cooked foods
is bread.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:03.510
The process of combining flour and yeast may
seem an unlikely discovery but yeast actually

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:09.629
grows naturally on cereal grain that is left
out, so it was one of those happy accidents,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:14.249
like when someone dropped a pig in maple syrup
and bacon pancakes were invented.&nbsp;

00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:19.609
The next step we needed to work out was how
to make food last a bit longer because chasing

00:02:19.609 --> 00:02:25.080
mammoths and foraging for berries every day
was tiring and we suspected that TV would

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.620
be invented soon, and we didn’t want to
be too busy chasing deer to be able to watch

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.620
it.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:34.669
There are numerous ways to preserve food and
different climates gave rise to different

00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:35.669
techniques.

00:02:35.669 --> 00:02:39.620
The main aim of preservation is to kill microbes
and to prevent them from having the moisture

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.629
they need to multiply.

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:46.549
In hot climates this could be done through
drying but it wasn’t so easy for us damp

00:02:46.549 --> 00:02:49.180
people far from the equator.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:54.010
Those in more moderate climates turned to
salting their food to preseve it.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:58.760
Osmosis is where the water inside a cell tries
to reach the same concentration as the water

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.260
outside the cell.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.590
So when you salt a meat, the moisture outside
the cells has more salt than the moisture

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:09.950
inside the cells, so the water is drawn out
through the semi-permeable cell wall to try

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.189
and balance it out.

00:03:11.189 --> 00:03:15.140
It’s a bit like communism, but with less
furry hats and more water.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:21.439
Other methods of preservation include smoking,
sugaring, pickling, jellying or just hiding

00:03:21.439 --> 00:03:24.980
you food somewhere were all the microbes can’t
find it.&nbsp;

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.889
With the rise of electricity, the refrigerator
became an everyday item for almost every household.

00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:35.099
The great thing about a fridge is that you
get the benefit of longer lasting food, without

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:40.169
having to dramatically alter its flavour or
texture, which most preservation techniques

00:03:40.169 --> 00:03:41.510
often do.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:47.189
It also enabled us to plan ahead, rather than
having to constantly buy things we needed

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:48.189
that day.&nbsp;

00:03:48.189 --> 00:03:52.760
Scottish physician William Cullen is considered
the founding father of the fridge since he

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:58.889
gave the first recorded demonstration of artificial
refrigeration back in 1756.

00:03:58.889 --> 00:04:03.120
The modern fridge works by pumping a coolant
through a series of evaporator and condenser

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.400
coils.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.290
Soon we will embrace the futuristic power
of the smart fridge, where you’ll be able

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:12.950
to text your device on your way home to see
if you’re out of milk and it’ll probably

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.589
start hiding the cheese if it thinks you’ve
put on a few pounds.

00:04:15.589 --> 00:04:18.000
You’re not the boss of me, fridge.&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.900
One of the most reliable and long-lasting
ways to preserve food is with canning, an

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:28.810
invention that largely came about thanks to
a game we like to play, called war.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:33.919
Humans love war, we play it all the time,
but it does make lunch rather complicated

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:38.340
when you’ve covered all the veg in mustard
gas and blown the legs off your chef.&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.669
Frenchman, Nicolas Appert, won a substantial
12,000 franc prize for developing a way of

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:49.090
sealing food in glass jars, to help the troops
in the Napoleonic wars.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:54.400
Canning slowly developed, although the lead
they used to solder the cans didn’t do people

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.370
much good, but it was about 100 years later,
when WW1 started, that it really took off.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:05.080
Unfortunately there weren’t enough tins
to keep the Brits happy and by 1917, British

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.630
soldiers were issued with cigarettes and amphetamines
to try to suppress their appetite.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:15.580
Both world wars had a major effect on food,
especially in Britain since we imported a

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.100
large percentage of our produce.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:24.900
Rationing during WW2 greatly reduced the availability
of a lot of products, so much so the many

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:31.099
children thought that fruit such as the banana
were fairy tale inventions that didn’t actually

00:05:31.099 --> 00:05:35.020
exist, since they had never seen one.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:40.659
Rationing went on until 1954 in the UK and
this is largely how the British ended up with

00:05:40.659 --> 00:05:45.690
such a poor culinary reputation around the
world; the food was incredibly boring because

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:51.180
there weren't many options and then it took
a long while for us to get out of the habit.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.610
Next up is not a technique but the one man
who has had the biggest influence on food

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:00.139
all around the world is Auguste Escoffier.

00:06:00.139 --> 00:06:04.180
His book Le Guide Culinaire (pron “Le Geed
Killinaire”), published in 1903, broke down

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.819
the methods and techniques that make up classic
cookery.

00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:11.879
It forms the basis of classic chef training
to this day.&nbsp;

00:06:11.879 --> 00:06:17.969
In 1890, Escoffier moved to London to take
control of the kitchens at the Savoy hotel.

00:06:17.969 --> 00:06:23.460
His system of organising a kitchen had a huge
impact on their safety and on the consistency

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:24.719
of the food.

00:06:24.719 --> 00:06:29.629
By breaking the cooking process into sections,
each chef became part of a team who oversaw

00:06:29.629 --> 00:06:34.610
only a few small parts of the meal, enabling
them to perfect it and repeat it.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:39.620
Before him, it had all been a bit hectic and
you’d be lucky to get a sandwich without

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:43.199
someone chopping off a finger and getting
hair in the butter.&nbsp;

00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:47.159
Dining at the Savoy became hugely popular
and was what started London’s restaurant

00:06:47.159 --> 00:06:53.509
culture, since before this, aristocratic families
would have their own chefs and eat in their

00:06:53.509 --> 00:06:54.509
grand houses.

00:06:54.509 --> 00:07:00.690
But with the draw of great cooks, hotels began
to get crowds of excited diners and so the

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.740
fine dining restaurants came after.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:09.660
Without Escoffier, the Emperor of Chefs, there’d
be no L'Enclume, no Gordon Ramsey and no cheeky

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.430
Nandos.&nbsp;

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.270
When your tummy is rumbling and you scroll
through all the things you could have; pizza,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:24.040
tacos, ramen, a kebab, a curry, sushi – you
soon realise that the one thing that effects

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:28.139
what we eat today, more than anything else,
is immigration.&nbsp;

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:32.939
There has certainly been a long history of
ingredients being discovered and included

00:07:32.939 --> 00:07:36.219
in national cuisines; just look at potatoes,
sugar, rice.

00:07:36.219 --> 00:07:41.969
But it is the people who bring the rich history
of the food and new ways of preparing it that

00:07:41.969 --> 00:07:43.759
really increase a countries eating vocabulary.

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:49.980
The Italians gave New York a lot of its famed
cuisine and try taking a Californian to a

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:54.940
Mexican restaurant outside their own state,
you’ll get more tutting than at a political

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.129
debate.

00:07:56.129 --> 00:08:01.120
Curry is now one of Great Britain’s leading
national dishes and the famous Chicken Tikka

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.060
Masala, one of Britians most eaten dishes,
was something invented here as part of the

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.819
influence of immigrants, but it's not actually
an Inidian dish.

00:08:09.819 --> 00:08:14.560
So who knows what exciting new dishes will
be invented in the next 10 years thanks to

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.150
the power of shared knowledge.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.340
Food preperation has been rather basic up
until now.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:26.139
It's only in recent years we've started experimenting
with how technology can shape our food, from

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:28.300
sous-vide to sperification.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:34.440
Perhaps in the future we’ll be able to grow
perfect steaks in jars and splice wheat DNA

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:38.190
with a deep-fat fryer so we can grow doughnuts
in our own garden.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:09:02.250
Well, one can dream 
at least.

